// RouteMate Transport Hub — PostgreSQL 18 / Prisma 7.10.0.
// Phase 2 native spatial types, GiST indexes, active uniqueness and RLS are
// maintained in 20260910150000_phase2_spatial_rls/migration.sql.
// See ../PHASE2.md for runtime roles, transaction context and migration workflow.
// Remaining production hardening items below are a backlog, not all implemented.
// 2. SQL migration enables and forces RLS. Derive tenant/user context from
//    authenticated server state, set transaction-local context, use USING and
//    WITH CHECK, and deny absent context. App roles must not own tables/BYPASSRLS.
//    Global passengers/sessions/contacts have owner policies; nullable-org
//    users, roles, notifications, audits/security have explicit platform rules.
//    Public QR/share endpoints must use constrained server queries/views, never
//    expose driver contacts, documents, national IDs or broad tenant access.
// 3. Active primary-driver and QR uniqueness are implemented. Remaining indexes:
//    current owner, crew/route assignments, primary trusted contact, open grants,
//    global role codes and global user email/phone (WHERE organization_id IS NULL).
//    Null scope columns need separate predicates / NULLS NOT DISTINCT on PG15+.
//    Time-dependent now() is not an immutable index predicate: check expiries at
//    runtime; use revoked/end/status fields and transactional lifecycle updates.
//    Full unique identifiers deliberately remain reserved after soft deletion.
// 4. CHECK constraints: nonnegative sizes/amounts/accuracy/distance/durations,
//    positive capacities, valid stop order/year, start < end, issue <= expiry,
//    expiry > issue/create, completion/return timestamps and status consistency;
//    dimension-specific driver history, recipient/reporter/author shapes, media
//    evidence shape, scope shape, login identifier availability. Pair anonymous
//    session and passenger identities only when their owner matches. Enforce
//    required reporter identity for complaint/lost-property, and Incident by type.
// 5. Composite (organizationId, foreignId) FKs already enforce tenant equality
//    between operational models. Remaining UUID-only user/role/global references
//    require authorization AND SQL triggers for tenant/platform exceptions.
//    Add acyclic parent hierarchy/type validation and org-unit/park scope checks.
//    Add same-vehicle/driver/route consistency triggers for scans/journeys and
//    denormalized case links; validate reporters own the referenced journey,
//    session and trusted contacts. Tenant equality alone is not authorization.
// 6. Range exclusion constraints prevent overlapping primary-driver/ownership
//    history; define half-open [start,end) intervals. Lock target rows for rotation,
//    reassignment, status/history and owner-cache updates. Freeze identity/start
//    fields of historical assignments once referenced; end/status may transition.
// 7. Append-only permissions/triggers for logs, scans, status/case histories,
//    journey events and evidence; no cascading deletes. Add database updated_at
//    triggers for non-Prisma writes (@updatedAt is Prisma-managed only). Retention
//    and controlled anonymization jobs are separate from ordinary application roles.
// 8. PostgreSQL full-text tsvector/GIN search, case-insensitive normalized identity
//    uniqueness, tenant-safe human code allocation, and operational retention/
//    partitioning are SQL/application work. No MAX(code)+1 code allocation.
// 9. Lifecycle transitions, feature flags, encryption, token/password hashing,
//    least-privilege public projections and notification delivery need application
//    implementation. This schema is a foundation, not a deployed security boundary.

